<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		The app ships Korean copy only, so the development region is ko. Every
		user-facing string in this file is Korean; the file is UTF-8 (declared
		above) which is what makes non-ASCII CFBundleDisplayName and usage
		descriptions render correctly instead of as mojibake.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>ko</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>곰신로그</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<!--
		CFBundleShortVersionString is MARKETING_VERSION and must equal `version`
		in package.json and android versionName.
		CFBundleVersion is CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION, the build number, bumped on
		every upload to App Store Connect and never reused.
		Both come from the Xcode build settings; `npm run verify:native`
		asserts the agreement. See docs/kiro/NATIVE_RELEASE_GUIDE.md.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>GomsinlogAppleIAPSaleEnabled</key>
	<string>$(GOMSINLOG_APPLE_IAP_NATIVE_SALE_ENABLED)</string>

	<!--
		OAuth return path. `authRedirectUrl()` in src/lib/platform.ts builds
		exactly `gomsinlog://auth/callback`, and src/lib/deepLinks.ts is what
		consumes it after @capacitor/app forwards
		application(_:open:options:).

		Exactly one scheme is registered, and it is not a scheme any other party
		could plausibly claim. `CFBundleURLName` is the reverse-DNS owner id;
		`LSHandlerRank` = Owner states this app is the canonical handler.

		iOS has no per-path filtering for custom schemes, unlike the Android
		intent-filter, which pins host and path, so deepLinks.ts is the layer
		that rejects any `gomsinlog://` URL that is not the callback route.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>app.gomsinlog.auth</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>gomsinlog</string>
			</array>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Owner</string>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
		Privacy usage descriptions. Korean, because the consent alert is shown to
		a Korean-speaking user, and specific about what the data is used for:
		a vague string is a review rejection.

		DELIBERATELY ABSENT since 2026-08-21: NSMicrophoneUsageDescription
			The voice recorder left the composer with the PRODUCT_V3 §12.4
			upload gate (photo-only until the P6 encrypted media foundation), so
			nothing calls getUserMedia({ audio }) and a usage string for an
			unused sensor would itself be a review flag. Returns with voice at
			P6, alongside the parked rationale in src/lib/nativePermissions.ts.

		NSCameraUsageDescription
			The "지금찍기" button sets capture="environment" on the file input.
			WKWebView offers "사진 촬영" for that input and presents the camera
			in-process, which requires this string. Removing the capture
			attribute would remove this requirement; that tradeoff is recorded
			in docs/kiro/NATIVE_RELEASE_GUIDE.md.

		DELIBERATELY ABSENT: NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription /
		NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription
			The gallery path in WKWebView goes through the out-of-process
			PHPicker / UIDocumentPicker, which hands back only the chosen file
			and needs no library authorisation. No code in this app reads the
			photo library, and nothing writes back to it.

		DELIBERATELY ABSENT: NSUserTrackingUsageDescription,
		NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription, NSContactsUsageDescription,
		NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription, NSFaceIDUsageDescription
			No corresponding API is called anywhere in the app or in the three
			iOS pods it links (@capacitor/ios, @capacitor/app,
			@capacitor/browser).
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>‘지금찍기’로 오늘의 순간을 바로 촬영할 때에만 카메라를 사용해요. 찍은 사진은 내가 저장을 눌렀을 때만 기록에 첨부돼요.</string>

	<!--
		App Transport Security is deliberately NOT configured. Omitting
		NSAppTransportSecurity leaves ATS at its secure defaults: TLS 1.2+,
		forward secrecy, no arbitrary loads. Supabase is https/wss only, so no
		exception is needed, and adding an empty dictionary here would only
		invite someone to relax it later.
	-->

	<!--
		Export compliance: the app uses only the platform's standard TLS through
		URLSession/WKWebView, which is exempt. Declaring it here removes the
		manual encryption question on every App Store Connect upload.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<!--
		iOS 27 requires the scene life cycle for apps built with the current SDK.
		The app remains single-window; Main.storyboard still instantiates the
		CAPBridgeViewController, while SceneDelegate forwards custom-scheme and
		universal-link callbacks that no longer arrive at AppDelegate.
	-->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
		<key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
		<dict>
			<key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
			<array>
				<dict>
					<key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
					<string>Default Configuration</string>
					<key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
					<string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
					<key>UISceneStoryboardFile</key>
					<string>Main</string>
				</dict>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</dict>
	<!--
		arm64, not the template's armv7: IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET is 14.0, and
		no 32-bit device can run iOS 11 or later. Leaving armv7 declares a
		capability no supported device has.
	-->
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<!--
		Portrait only on iPhone, matching "orientation": "portrait" in
		public/manifest.json and the fixed bottom tab bar in
		src/components/MobileShell.tsx. iPad keeps every orientation because the
		system requires a resizable universal app to accept them.
	-->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<!--
		UIUserInterfaceStyle is intentionally absent so the app follows the
		system appearance. src/index.css drives light/dark from
		`prefers-color-scheme` and the token set guarded by
		src/lib/themeTokens.test.ts; pinning a style here would strand dark-mode
		users on the light palette.

		UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance stays true so Capacitor's
		bridge view controller decides the status bar style per appearance
		instead of a single global value that would be unreadable in one theme.
	-->
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
